1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,846
The CEO of the world's most hyped AI
company is stressed.

2
00:00:03,886 --> 00:00:06,392
Because his ChatGPT has reached almost
every home...

3
00:00:06,432 --> 00:00:08,534
...and yet the company keeps losing money.

4
00:00:10,274 --> 00:00:11,534
Welcome to the AI era...

5
00:00:11,574 --> 00:00:15,310
...where a simple chatbot turned into a $7
trillion gamble almost overnight.

6
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,357
30 November 2022. A Wednesday.

7
00:00:18,397 --> 00:00:20,473
At one company, everything was normal.

8
00:00:20,513 --> 00:00:25,267
People were drinking coffee, sharing
memes, developers were fixing bugs.

9
00:00:25,307 --> 00:00:27,865
And that same day, OpenAI dropped its
chatbot... ChatGPT.

10
00:00:30,031 --> 00:00:33,257
Within 24 hours, the internet had a new
obsession.

11
00:00:33,297 --> 00:00:37,990
Someone was writing emails with it,
someone was fixing code, and someone was
writing their breakup story.

12
00:00:38,030 --> 00:00:42,760
SON: Dad, I'm talking to an AI bot... that
can make us crorepatis!

13
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:43,860
DAD: How?

14
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:48,967
SON (typing): "Build me a ₹100 crore
business in 1 minute. 100% profit.

15
00:00:49,007 --> 00:00:50,190
And no mistakes."

16
00:00:50,230 --> 00:00:54,568
ChatGPT went so viral that it got 1
million users in just 5 days.

17
00:00:54,608 --> 00:00:56,703
And 100 million in 2 months.

18
00:00:56,743 --> 00:01:00,332
Instagram took two and a half years to
reach 100 million users.

19
00:01:00,372 --> 00:01:02,060
Facebook took four and a half.

20
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,420
ChatGPT became... the fastest-growing app
in history.

21
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:09,340
Then... Google's headquarters went into
full panic mode.

22
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:13,820
BOSS: This technology is ours! We built
the Transformer model! What happened to
it?

23
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:17,390
BOSS: Then why is my own son using ChatGPT
instead of Google's AI?

24
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:20,390
ENGINEER: Because... we were scared to
launch it.

25
00:01:20,430 --> 00:01:21,861
BOSS: Then launch anything now!

26
00:01:21,901 --> 00:01:24,518
Call it Bard, call it Gemini, call it
whatever you want.

27
00:01:24,558 --> 00:01:25,230
Just launch it!

28
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:28,507
Google went into full hustle mode.

29
00:01:28,547 --> 00:01:29,607
It had the technology for years.

30
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:36,092
But the company was scared... that the AI
might say something wrong, there might be
a lawsuit, or its name might get dragged
in the media.

31
00:01:36,132 --> 00:01:38,929
And OpenAI? It launched without fear.

32
00:01:38,969 --> 00:01:42,392
Within weeks, Microsoft put $10 billion
into OpenAI.

33
00:01:42,432 --> 00:01:45,455
And kept another $3 billion ready on the
side.

34
00:01:45,495 --> 00:01:47,900
Google rushed to launch Bard...

35
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:51,390
USER: Gemini, tell me, who is the most
popular person in the world?

36
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:52,490
GEMINI: Modi Ji.

37
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:54,550
- USER: Who is Modi Ji?
- GEMINI: The Modi government.

38
00:01:54,590 --> 00:01:57,980
And that's it... on day one, Gemini made a
complete mess.

39
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,760
On the other side, Amazon started pouring
billions into Anthropic.

40
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,600
And this was the investors' scene...

41
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:05,643
STARTUP: Our app takes dogs for walks...

42
00:02:05,683 --> 00:02:08,860
...but we use machine learning to pick the
best route.

43
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:10,910
INVESTOR: Shh! Here's $50 million.

44
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:13,250
STARTUP: Won't you look at our demo?

45
00:02:13,290 --> 00:02:18,350
PITCHER (2023): We're building an app that
lets you talk directly to Voldemort... in
Python.

46
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:19,450
JUDGE: Next, please.

47
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:20,890
PITCHER: We're using AI in it.

48
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:23,260
- JUDGE: AI? Really?
- PITCHER: Yes.

49
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:24,930
JUDGE: Here's $100 million.

50
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:28,805
One company even raised $30 million to
build an AI...

51
00:02:28,845 --> 00:02:35,570
...whose job was to give back another AI's
answers under a new name.

52
00:02:35,610 --> 00:02:40,225
But the real madness was... the GPU
shortage.

53
00:02:40,265 --> 00:02:43,531
These tiny computer chips suddenly became
more valuable than gold and oil.

54
00:02:43,571 --> 00:02:45,920
And Nvidia became the most valuable
company in the world.

55
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:50,575
Nvidia's CEO Jensen Huang started showing
up at conferences in a leather jacket...
like a rockstar.

56
00:02:50,615 --> 00:02:53,940
And in a way... he was one.

57
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,525
Renting one high-end GPU cost about $3 an
hour.

58
00:02:55,565 --> 00:02:56,625
Sounds cheap, right?

59
00:02:56,665 --> 00:02:59,889
But a startup's work was never going to
run on one GPU...

60
00:02:59,929 --> 00:03:04,798
Even a small startup may rent 500 chips
just to train one model.

61
00:03:04,838 --> 00:03:07,314
That's $1,500 an hour.

62
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:09,252
$36,000 a day.

63
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:13,776
And over $1.1 million a month. Just in GPU
rent.

64
00:03:13,816 --> 00:03:19,884
Still, companies were hoarding GPUs... as
if the world was ending and people were
stocking up on rations.

65
00:03:19,924 --> 00:03:23,874
As fast as Nvidia made chips, demand grew
even faster.

66
00:03:23,914 --> 00:03:26,754
At one point, the waiting list reached 12
months.

67
00:03:26,794 --> 00:03:28,115
A black market started.

68
00:03:28,155 --> 00:03:30,550
People began smuggling these chips across
borders.

69
00:03:30,590 --> 00:03:33,921
By 2024, everything had become
"AI-powered".

70
00:03:33,961 --> 00:03:38,006
Your toaster could now burn bread 10% more
efficiently.

71
00:03:38,046 --> 00:03:41,560
Your calendar app was suggesting meeting
times with an algorithm.

72
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,731
And the supermarket self-checkout? The
camera was always there...

73
00:03:44,771 --> 00:03:47,333
...it just got renamed "machine learning".

74
00:03:47,373 --> 00:03:48,540
The party was in full swing.

75
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,416
Champagne was popping. Cheques were
clearing.

76
00:03:51,456 --> 00:03:53,955
Then someone asked that awkward
question...

77
00:03:53,995 --> 00:03:56,430
"Bro... how much does it cost to run all
this?"

78
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:58,222
January 2025.

79
00:03:58,262 --> 00:04:02,829
Sam Altman posted a tweet that sent
investors' blood pressure through the
roof.

80
00:04:02,869 --> 00:04:06,196
"We're losing money on OpenAI Pro
subscriptions.

81
00:04:06,236 --> 00:04:09,143
People use it much more than we expected."

82
00:04:09,183 --> 00:04:10,862
Yes, you heard that right.

83
00:04:10,902 --> 00:04:14,032
OpenAI charges $200 a month for its Pro
plan.

84
00:04:14,072 --> 00:04:16,991
And still loses money on every subscriber.

85
00:04:17,031 --> 00:04:19,377
But wait... the story gets worse.

86
00:04:19,417 --> 00:04:27,059
Look at OpenAI's numbers for 2024.
Revenue: $3.7 billion. Loss: $5 billion.

87
00:04:27,099 --> 00:04:32,049
The company didn't just fail to make a
profit... it burned more than it earned.

88
00:04:32,089 --> 00:04:33,991
Now comes the really shocking part.

89
00:04:34,031 --> 00:04:42,140
According to one research firm, running AI
and its data centres will need $6.7
trillion by 2030.

90
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:43,798
Let's break that down.

91
00:04:43,838 --> 00:04:51,784
$5.2 trillion goes straight into AI
infrastructure: GPUs, chips, power,
cooling and building data centres.

92
00:04:51,824 --> 00:04:55,419
The remaining $1.5 trillion goes into
cloud infrastructure.

93
00:04:55,459 --> 00:05:02,309
And just the four biggest tech companies
were expected to spend $350 billion in
2025.

94
00:05:02,349 --> 00:05:03,282
In one year.

95
00:05:03,322 --> 00:05:09,915
To justify this spending, AI companies
would need to earn $2 trillion every year
by 2030.

96
00:05:09,955 --> 00:05:13,961
And the whole AI industry's revenue today?
About $200 billion.

97
00:05:14,001 --> 00:05:16,300
So revenue has to grow 10 times.

98
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:21,303
BOARD MEMBER: Sam, by our numbers revenue
will be $20 billion in 2025.

99
00:05:21,343 --> 00:05:25,020
And you're saying you'll reach $100
billion by 2029?

100
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:27,000
SAM: We need to grow at exactly this
speed.

101
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:29,244
BOARD MEMBER: Sam, that's not speed.

102
00:05:29,284 --> 00:05:33,794
That's a hockey-stick graph drawn by
someone who has never played hockey.

103
00:05:33,834 --> 00:05:40,650
- BOARD MEMBER: Do you know how much just trying will cost?
- SAM: $115 billion of cash burn by 2029.

104
00:05:40,690 --> 00:05:47,500
- BOARD MEMBER: And when will profit come?
- SAM: In 2029. Maybe 2030. Or 2031?

105
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:50,080
BOARD MEMBER: And if revenue doesn't reach
$100 billion?

106
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:51,600
What if your hockey stick breaks?

107
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:53,160
SAM: Then we'll raise more money.

108
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:56,860
BOARD MEMBER: From whom? Who's left?
Microsoft? Saudi Arabia?

109
00:05:56,900 --> 00:05:59,650
Some pension fund that's a bit too
optimistic?

110
00:05:59,690 --> 00:06:02,330
SAM: Come on, the world isn't short of
investors!

111
00:06:02,370 --> 00:06:07,345
Since 2021, private investors have poured
hundreds of billions of dollars into AI
startups.

112
00:06:07,385 --> 00:06:13,048
Most of it went to just 10 companies,
whose total value grew by about $1
trillion.

113
00:06:13,088 --> 00:06:17,200
Everyone was making the same bet... that
one of them would become the next Google.

114
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,009
Then the cracks started to show.

115
00:06:19,049 --> 00:06:21,326
ChatGPT started to "hallucinate".

116
00:06:21,366 --> 00:06:25,679
In tech language, that means... making
things up with complete confidence.

117
00:06:25,719 --> 00:06:28,884
Citing research papers that don't exist.

118
00:06:28,924 --> 00:06:31,161
Court cases that never happened.

119
00:06:31,201 --> 00:06:34,489
And numbers that probably came from
another planet.

120
00:06:34,529 --> 00:06:39,789
Google's AI told a user to add glue to
pizza so the cheese would stick.

121
00:06:39,829 --> 00:06:43,288
It learned that from a joke on Reddit.
Yes, really.

122
00:06:43,328 --> 00:06:44,603
Then the lawsuits began.

123
00:06:44,643 --> 00:06:51,842
Writers, artists, journalists... all had
the same question: "Did you train your AI
on my copyrighted work?"

124
00:06:51,882 --> 00:06:54,016
The answer? "Umm... yes."

125
00:06:54,056 --> 00:06:58,685
The New York Times sued OpenAI. Getty
Images sued Stability AI.

126
00:06:58,725 --> 00:07:02,890
Writers dragged companies to court.
Artists went after the rest.

127
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:07,180
AI COMPANY: We didn't steal. We trained
our AI on public data.

128
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:12,460
ARTIST: You took the whole internet's data
without permission... and made money from
it.

129
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:16,040
AI COMPANY: Well... when you put it like
that, it does sound illegal.

130
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:18,674
By mid-2024, cracks had started to appear.

131
00:07:18,714 --> 00:07:21,783
The hype was very loud. But the numbers
were scary.

132
00:07:21,823 --> 00:07:27,541
And now one question was buzzing in
everyone's head... just like a mosquito
near your ear at 3 AM:

133
00:07:27,581 --> 00:07:28,650
"When will this bubble burst?"

134
00:07:28,690 --> 00:07:31,653
The losses are huge. Company valuations
look far bigger than reality.

135
00:07:31,693 --> 00:07:38,072
But this bubble isn't bursting the way the
dot-com bubble did. Why?

136
00:07:38,112 --> 00:07:39,172
Because the people inflating it have the
deepest pockets in the world.

137
00:07:39,212 --> 00:07:45,110
In 1999, startups had zero revenue... and
17 foosball tables in the office for fun.

138
00:07:45,150 --> 00:07:51,178
Today, the big tech companies funding the
AI bubble actually make money.

139
00:07:51,218 --> 00:07:52,189
A lot of it.

140
00:07:52,229 --> 00:07:55,134
So much that, if they wanted... they could
buy a whole country.

141
00:07:55,174 --> 00:07:56,565
Microsoft makes billions from Azure.

142
00:07:56,605 --> 00:07:59,065
Google makes billions from ads.

143
00:07:59,105 --> 00:08:06,378
Meta makes billions showing you the same
meme... your cousin already sent you on
WhatsApp.

144
00:08:06,418 --> 00:08:10,838
These big companies aren't investing in AI
because it's profitable right now.

145
00:08:10,878 --> 00:08:15,150
They're investing... because they're
scared of being left behind.

146
00:08:15,190 --> 00:08:18,939
What if AI becomes the next iPhone moment?
The next Google moment?

147
00:08:18,979 --> 00:08:22,575
What if this is the moment the whole world
changes overnight?

148
00:08:22,615 --> 00:08:26,403
Then the company that slows down...
becomes the next BlackBerry.

149
00:08:26,443 --> 00:08:28,437
And nobody wants to be BlackBerry.

150
00:08:28,477 --> 00:08:33,792
Think of these big tech companies as poker
players sitting at the table with
unlimited money.

151
00:08:33,832 --> 00:08:38,746
If Microsoft backs off, Google wins. If
Google backs off, Amazon wins.

152
00:08:38,786 --> 00:08:40,940
If Amazon backs off, Meta wins.

153
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:45,650
Nobody is blinking. Nobody is folding.
Everyone keeps raising the bet.

154
00:08:45,690 --> 00:08:48,865
And this is where the really tough
question comes in.

155
00:08:48,905 --> 00:08:52,261
If everyone keeps spending... how does the
bubble burst?

156
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:58,817
Short answer: when a real-world event
forces everyone... to stop pretending.

157
00:08:58,857 --> 00:09:02,196
Historically, such events come in two
kinds.

158
00:09:02,236 --> 00:09:05,073
One: the money suddenly stops coming.

159
00:09:05,113 --> 00:09:09,425
The dot-com bubble didn't burst because
building websites was a bad idea.

160
00:09:09,465 --> 00:09:12,558
It burst because the money tap was shut.

161
00:09:12,598 --> 00:09:16,173
Interest rates rose. Loans got expensive.
Funding vanished.

162
00:09:16,213 --> 00:09:19,109
And thousands of companies died overnight.

163
00:09:19,149 --> 00:09:26,813
If interest rates rise in the coming years
and big tech tightens its budgets... the
AI bubble's game is over instantly.

164
00:09:26,853 --> 00:09:30,972
Two: a huge, embarrassing failure comes
out.

165
00:09:31,012 --> 00:09:36,667
Every bubble has that moment. A failure so
big that everyone panics.

166
00:09:36,707 --> 00:09:39,717
In the dot-com era, company results
crushed expectations.

167
00:09:39,757 --> 00:09:41,748
In 2008, it was Lehman Brothers.

168
00:09:41,788 --> 00:09:43,627
In crypto, it was FTX.

169
00:09:43,667 --> 00:09:47,979
For AI, it could be a big lab revealing a
massive loss.

170
00:09:48,019 --> 00:09:53,360
A new model's disastrous launch. Or a
lawsuit that stops everything.

171
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,059
So where do we stand right now?

172
00:09:55,099 --> 00:09:57,623
Big tech is still spending.

173
00:09:57,663 --> 00:10:03,186
No result has caused an earthquake yet. No
FTX-level scam has come out.

174
00:10:03,226 --> 00:10:06,921
Cracks are showing... but not big enough
to break trust.

175
00:10:06,961 --> 00:10:11,613
So yes, this bubble is inflated. Yes, the
numbers are insane.

176
00:10:11,653 --> 00:10:14,614
But it won't burst just because of hype
and noise.

177
00:10:14,654 --> 00:10:20,338
It will burst when one of these giants
finally says... "Okay. That's enough."

178
00:10:20,378 --> 00:10:22,849
Until then, the bubble will keep floating.

179
00:10:22,889 --> 00:10:28,733
We're not at the explosion yet. We're at
the point where the tension keeps
building.

180
00:10:28,773 --> 00:10:31,794
The breath you take in before a scream.

181
00:10:31,834 --> 00:10:34,021
The pull on a rope just before it snaps.

182
00:10:34,061 --> 00:10:37,050
The episode right before the season
finale.

